From 583de6afff6d0a7bfec5786d52e038400336c442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 22:36:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/79] util/log: include u_process.h for the use that is not
 POSIX-guarded
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa_log_init_once calls util_get_process_name() under

  #if !DETECT_OS_WINDOWS

but the include that declares it, "util/u_process.h", sits under

  #if DETECT_OS_POSIX

Those are not the same condition. A platform that is neither POSIX nor
Windows compiles the call with no declaration in scope, which on any
recent GCC is an error, and the implicit int return then makes the
adjacent snprintf's %s take an int.

The header is platform-independent — it includes only <stdbool.h> and
<stddef.h>, and u_process.c is built unconditionally with its own
fallbacks — so it moves to the unconditional includes. <syslog.h>, which
really is POSIX, stays where it was.

This is a pre-existing bug, not something Horizon introduces; Horizon is
just the first platform to be neither.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 3 item 3 (newlib/libnx gaps)
Why: no DETECT_OS_* macro matches Horizon, so DETECT_OS_POSIX and
  DETECT_OS_WINDOWS are both 0 and the mismatch is reachable.
Evidence: building src/util for aarch64/horizon fails with
    log.c:153:77: error: implicit declaration of function
                  'util_get_process_name'
    log.c:153:59: error: format '%s' expects argument of type 'char *',
                  but argument 4 has type 'int' [-Werror=format=]
Upstream: yes — it is a straightforward guard mismatch and the fix is
  a no-op everywhere the guards already agreed.
---
 src/util/log.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/util/log.c b/src/util/log.c
index b594a8f..981d28f 100644
--- a/src/util/log.c
+++ b/src/util/log.c
@@ -30,10 +30,12 @@
 #include "util/log.h"
 #include "util/ralloc.h"
 #include "util/u_debug.h"
+/* Used under #if !DETECT_OS_WINDOWS below, which is a wider condition
+ * than DETECT_OS_POSIX. The header itself is platform-independent. */
+#include "util/u_process.h"
 
 #if DETECT_OS_POSIX
 #include <syslog.h>
-#include "util/u_process.h"
 #endif
 
 #if DETECT_OS_ANDROID
-- 
